parts=[]
def add(s):parts.append(s)
add('''<h1>Predicting Perceived Intelligence from Design, Behavior and User Judgments</h1>
<p class="subtitle">A reproducible secondary analysis of four human studies published in 2026</p>
<p class="meta">Research manuscript · 4 November 2026 · Exploratory secondary analysis · No new participant recruitment</p>
<h2>Abstract</h2>
<p>Voice assistants can feel competent without providing reliable task evidence, and successful interactions can receive poor subjective evaluations. Existing research already models these perceptions; the practical question is what information a predictive model actually needs. We develop and evaluate a contextual evidence-and-attribution specification that keeps design assignments, reported user attributes, recorded behavior and concurrent subjective judgments separate. We analyze 553 released eligible observers from two voice-assistant vignette experiments, 148 users of a text co-creation system, and 240 participants in a text-LLM latency experiment. These are 941 participant records across four studies, not one harmonized sample. Intelligence and credibility are strongly associated in the voice studies (HTMT 0.847), while two-factor models retain poor absolute fit. Study-held-out design-plus-user prediction explains only 2.2% of intelligence variance in the forward transfer; contemporaneous credibility explains 51.8%. In co-creation, nested ridge and nonlinear models using available interaction logs give little improvement in point estimates over the training mean, whereas same-survey trust predicts about half the variance. In the text-latency data, event counts weakly predict thoughtfulness and usefulness, while other concurrent ratings give R² near 0.50–0.55. Manipulation-check filtering changes the pooled voice role contrast from −0.055 to +0.410 points under a first-check rule, demonstrating estimand sensitivity. The contribution is a new reproducible analysis of measurement overlap, information availability and transfer limits, not a new intelligence scale or universally validated voice metric. Live voice behavior, objective task correctness and direct intelligence ratings are not jointly observed; prospective validation remains necessary.</p>
<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> perceived intelligence; voice user interfaces; human–AI interaction; measurement validity; cross-study prediction; secondary analysis; reproducibility.</p>''')

add(f'''<h2>1. Introduction</h2>
<p>A useful evaluation model must distinguish what an assistant did from what a person inferred about it. Factual correctness, task completion, response timing, clarification and repair are observable aspects of an interaction. Perceived intelligence is an attribution made by a person. Credibility, usefulness, satisfaction and willingness to rely on the assistant are related outcomes, but substituting one for another changes the research question. This distinction matters when teams optimize a score: a predictor that needs a second questionnaire may reconstruct the first questionnaire very accurately while offering little guidance about which system behavior to change.</p>
<p>The broad idea of modeling subjective quality from interaction features is established. PARADISE linked task success and dialogue costs to satisfaction, SASSI identified multiple speech-interface dimensions, and contemporary signaling accounts already connect assistant features, attributed intelligence and evaluations. {cite('walker1997','hone2000','guha2023')} Our aim is therefore deliberately narrower than proposing the first model of assistant “smartness.” We ask how much predictive evidence comes from different information sources, whether neighboring subjective constructs are empirically separable, and how conclusions change under sample selection and external testing.</p>
<p>We contribute three linked empirical results. First, we audit direct intelligence measures against credibility and trust, retaining poor fit and instrument discrepancies rather than declaring independent dimensions. Second, we compare participant- and study-held-out prediction under explicit information constraints, including a nonlinear sensitivity model. Third, we reproduce the source voice study's selected-sample descriptive results and quantify the change associated with filtering on post-treatment role recognition. All analysis uses real, publicly released human observations; no agent-generated dialogue, LLM judge or synthetic participant supplies an empirical outcome.</p>
<p>The scope is consequential. The voice data are evaluations of recorded interactions, not live spoken sessions. The other datasets contain actual human–AI interaction but use text. We do not pool incompatible outcomes, transfer their coefficients to a voice product, or represent the resulting manuscript as a completed prospective voice experiment. The original broad project is advanced by a finished secondary study and a testable model specification, while its strongest live-interaction claims remain open.</p>

<h2>2. Related work and novelty boundary</h2>
<h3>2.1 Measurement precedes model fitting</h3>
<p>SASSI, SUISQ and Godspeed demonstrate why an overall impression should not automatically be treated as one interchangeable quality variable. Godspeed directly measures perceived intelligence alongside other attributions; SUISQ concerns speech-service quality. Their measures answer different questions and need contextual validation. {cite('hone2000','polkosky2005','lewis2015','bartneck2009')} More recent work includes perceived-intelligence models for voice shopping and a travel-assistant scale with conversational intelligence, information quality and anthropomorphism. {cite('bawack2021','ling2025')}</p>
<p>The Partner Modelling Questionnaire measures competence/dependability, human-likeness and flexibility. Its validation and cross-cultural adaptations are particularly relevant to “understands me,” but a factor that performs adequately in one setting need not transfer unchanged across languages. {cite('doyle2025','seaborn2024')} Consequently, our use of different item sets in different datasets supplies complementary evidence, not measurement invariance or a newly validated Russian questionnaire.</p>
<p>A targeted follow-up found Moussawi and Koufaris's full 2019 scale-development paper, including an independent survey validation sample. Its intelligence items already span speed, understanding, communication, processing and useful answers. Independent validation and multidimensional operationalization therefore cannot be claimed as our inventions; its survey nomological analysis differs from our held-out information-source contrasts. {cite('moussawi2019')} Zhao and colleagues' primary abstract reports a four-dimensional intelligence model using SEM and fsQCA, and Krämer and colleagues report a six-wave longitudinal study of trust and satisfaction. Their detailed full methods remained inaccessible in our follow-up, so they remain explicit limits on any priority claim. {cite('zhao2024','kraemer2026')}</p>
<h3>2.2 Behavior, context and perceived capability</h3>
<p>Guha and colleagues already treat observable features as signals of intelligence and artificiality, with user moderators. Xiao and Zhang's 2026 study links control variety and complexity to intelligence judgments across Alexa skills. {cite('guha2023','xiao2026')} Neither a larger path diagram nor new names for these mechanisms would establish novelty. Our information-source comparisons operationalize a different question: what prediction remains possible when other subjective evaluations are unavailable?</p>
<p>Contemporary experiments also discourage universal cue rules. Tan and colleagues study delay and task type in text interactions; Maslych and colleagues study fillers during delayed embodied dialogue; Jacka and colleagues examine speech disfluency and partner models. The studies differ in setting, intervention and endpoint. {cite('tan2026','maslych2025','jacka2025')} We therefore do not encode “faster always feels smarter” or “fluent always means competent” as fixed assumptions. A 2026 older-adult preprint and recent in-home qualitative work further motivate distinguishing personality, capability, trust and task context. {cite('mathur2026','frehmann2026')}</p>
<h3>2.3 Current automatic evaluation</h3>
<p>USR and FED establish multidimensional dialogue evaluation; SPUR and PRAISE develop interpretable satisfaction prediction. These are substantive comparators, but satisfaction labels from text dialogue are not direct intelligence measurements. {cite('mehri2020usr','mehri2020fed','lin2024','kim2025')} We do not compare our R² against published scores on incompatible benchmark tasks or claim state-of-the-art performance.</p>
<p>Novikova and colleagues already predict direct robot-intelligence ratings from linguistic, task and multimodal features in live spoken interaction, using random forests and a held-out split. This is a closer predecessor than satisfaction-only prediction and prevents any claim of the first interaction-feature predictor of intelligence. Their embodied setting and available features differ from our studies. {cite('novikova2017')}</p>
<p>Ma and colleagues' CHI 2026 work already predicts voice-interface UX from speech features in a 49-person study. Its jointly manipulated personas and evaluation protocol delimit causal and generalization claims. {cite('ma2026')} Recent primary industry reports, including DIAL and VOICE-H, provide larger human evaluations of voice interaction or speech output, but their aggregate dashboards and endpoints do not supply a validated individual intelligence target for our analysis. {cite('dial2026','voiceh2026')} TRACE/SCOPE also separates success from perceived experience, but its synthesized conversations cannot substitute for human observations here. {cite('hou2025')}</p>
<p>Our literature search used primary publisher pages, ACL Anthology, arXiv, author and university repositories, and exact-title/backward searches, with a cutoff of 4 November 2026. The companion review records access status, queries and unresolved leads. This is a targeted scoping review, not an exhaustive database search with a PRISMA denominator. The contribution that survives the inspected prior work is the specific cross-study evidence audit and its findings. Worldwide priority, exhaustive novelty and journal acceptance are not established.</p>''')

add('''<h2>3. Contextual evidence-and-attribution model</h2>
<p>Let D denote assigned design conditions, B observed interaction behavior, U reported user characteristics, C scenario or study context, I latent perceived intelligence, and T neighboring subjective evaluations. A future interaction model would use D, B and U to predict I while allowing context-specific coefficients. A separate measurement layer links I to ordinal questionnaire responses. Correlations between I and T are estimated rather than constrained to zero. This is a model specification built from established measurement and prediction methods; its conceptual components are not claimed as new theory.</p>
<p class="equation">Measurement: P(Y_ij ≤ k | I_i, T_i) = logistic(τ_jk − λ_j I_i − γ_j T_i).</p>
<p class="equation">Structural specification: I_i = α_C + β_D,C D_i + β_B,C B_i + β_U,C U_i + b_participant + ε_i.</p>
<p>The full ordinal hierarchical specification is not identifiable from all available releases: there are no repeated scenario-specific intelligence outcomes in the voice table and no common instrument across modalities. The implemented estimable model uses the mean of explicitly defined intelligence items as its target, a ridge regression for prediction, continuous-item covariance factor models as measurement diagnostics, and a single-item ordered-logit sensitivity analysis in the voice data. The prospective protocol describes how to estimate the richer model from new live sessions. These levels of completion are kept separate.</p>
<p>Three information sets define the empirical comparison. The design/user set contains assigned conditions and reported attributes. The interaction set adds only available logs, such as counts and session duration; it does not invent accuracy, response latency or semantic quality. The diagnostic survey set includes other contemporaneous ratings. The latter is a benchmark for concurrent association, not a deployable upper bound or causal mediator estimate. End-of-session adoption is separately marked because it may follow, rather than cause, a competence judgment.</p>
<p class="equation">Ridge: minimize Σ_i (Y_i − α − X_i β)² + λ ||β||², with λ chosen inside training data only.</p>
<p>We define the predictive gap as the difference between held-out performance using concurrent ratings and performance using design/user or behavioral evidence. Its interpretation is descriptive: it measures dependence of predictive performance on the available information. It does not identify common-method bias, a psychological mechanism, or an unattainable limit on prediction from richer logs. A latent halo, genuine correlated perceptions, omitted behavior and measurement noise can all contribute.</p>
<p>Five exploratory questions guide the analysis: (E1) how separable are intelligence and adjacent judgments; (E2) does design/user prediction transfer between voice experiments; (E3) how sensitive is the role contrast to manipulation-check selection; (E4) do observed co-creation logs add predictive value; and (E5) do latency and task effects depend on the subjective outcome? The analysis specification was written after source inspection, not externally preregistered. Later fixes and the reviewer-requested nonlinear model are documented in the audit record.</p>''')

add(f'''<h2>4. Data, provenance and estimands</h2>
<p>We selected public datasets accompanying 2026 publications with human ratings, interpretable design or behavioral variables, and verifiable primary sources. Selection was opportunistic and access-constrained, not a representative sampling frame of assistants. The analysis date is not the data-collection date. Older commercial voice stimuli and a particular text model remain part of these recent publications.</p>
<p><strong>Voice vignettes.</strong> Sarigül, Schneider and Utz provide two experiments of role framing and, in the first experiment, speech style, with UK native-English-speaking Prolific participants. We use 353 and 200 released eligible respondents. Five intelligence and five credibility items are rated on seven-category scales. Six scenarios are reflected in a final evaluation, not six independently labeled rows. Earlier technical exclusions from the second experiment are unavailable. The full-release estimate therefore is not strict intention-to-treat over all recruited participants. {cite('sarigul2026')}</p>
<p><strong>Text co-creation.</strong> Komura and Yamada release questionnaires, session metrics and note-level records for 148 Japanese Yahoo Crowdsourcing participants interacting with GPT-4.1 on a brainstorming canvas. The source recruited in May 2025. Adapted five-category intelligence items and 0–7 trust items are available. Six assignments are recorded as unknown: measurement uses all 148, primary condition-aware prediction uses 142, and an all-148 prediction sensitivity is retained. Adoption is a recorded choice, not independently verified usefulness or correctness. {cite('komura2026')}</p>
<p><strong>Text latency.</strong> Tan and colleagues release 2,651 events from 240 US Prolific participants using a custom GPT-4o interface, assigned to 2/9/20-second delays and Creation/Advice tasks. Seven quality ratings occur once per participant. Three IDs have conflicting task labels among their event rows. Primary inference uses the survey-bearing row; a sensitivity excludes those IDs. Prediction uses the unambiguous 237. These outcomes contain no direct intelligence item. The source already excluded 63 participants using a post-assignment latency-fidelity threshold; their records are absent. These contrasts concern the released sample, not a recovered full intention-to-treat effect. {cite('tan2026')}</p>''')
add(table(['Source','Released units','Analyzed endpoint','Validation boundary'],[
    ['Voice experiment 1','353 observers','Intelligence, credibility (1–7)','Recorded voice vignettes'],
    ['Voice experiment 2','200 observers','Intelligence, credibility (1–7)','Held-out related experiment'],
    ['Text co-creation','148 users; 142 known assignments','Adapted intelligence (1–5)','Participant and condition holdouts'],
    ['Text latency','240 users; 237 unambiguous','Thoughtfulness, usefulness (1–7)','Participant CV; separate endpoints'],
], 'Table 1. Real human data used. Counts are records across studies; no common-scale pooling is performed.'))
add('''<p>Raw files remain unchanged and are identified by SHA256 and source URLs. The SPSS-to-CSV export preserves numeric values; the original codebooks are retained. Intelligence composites in the voice release exactly equal their five-item means. In co-creation, all selected-note totals reconcile across the detail and summary sheets. The file has 5,849 notes, one more than the published text, and anonymized note IDs collide; the unique key is session × creator × turn. Each session has exactly 20 AI notes, so that count is uninformative and excluded from final predictors.</p>
<p>Instrument differences are material. The co-creation scale includes responsiveness and omits the usual sensible item; its supplementary likeability block also repeats an adjective pair. The voice preregistration and final instrument differ in one adjective label. We follow the final released instruments and do not harmonize scores through simple rescaling. The original studies' ethics and recruitment apply to their observations. No new human-study approval or consent collection is claimed for this secondary work. PLOS materials and the latency repository carry CC BY terms; the voice repository exposes no explicit blanket license, so its raw data are not relicensed by this project.</p>''')

add(f'''<h2>5. Analysis and verification</h2>
<h3>5.1 Measurement diagnostics</h3>
<p>We calculate internal consistency, composite associations, HTMT with 2,000 participant bootstrap draws, and one-factor versus correlated-factor covariance models. HTMT and fit indices are diagnostics, not universal pass/fail proofs of separability. Modern methodological work warns against automatic discriminant-validity conclusions from a single cutoff. {cite('henseler2015','ronkko2022')} Covariance models treat ordinal items as approximately continuous; no robust ordinal CFA, scalar invariance or longitudinal invariance was completed. We retain model misfit and do not add residual correlations after inspecting modification indices. Information criteria are calculated relative to the same saturated-model constant and compared only within sample and item set.</p>
<h3>5.2 Prediction under information constraints</h3>
<p>Voice models train on experiment 1 and test on experiment 2, then reverse direction using the assertive arm of experiment 1 to match the style supported in experiment 2. Testing the reverse model on all experiment-1 participants is a shifted-style sensitivity, not a third independent replication. User features are age as 2024 minus reported birth year, source gender indicators, smart-speaker ownership and mean reported information-search frequency across five named services. Ridge penalties are selected by five-fold training-only CV minimizing MAE. Imputation and scaling are inside the pipeline. Nonowners have structurally missing service-frequency responses; these remain missing until training-only median imputation with missingness indicators.</p>
<p>A subsequent voice sensitivity resamples training and test participants independently 1,000 times per transfer and fully refits both compared models, including penalty selection. Bootstrap copies of the same original participant are assigned to one inner-validation group using GroupKFold. This avoids duplicate leakage, although it changes the original shuffled-KFold tuning protocol. We therefore also fit a grouped-CV reference on the original samples. Percentile intervals incorporate empirical training and test variability; they do not account for new stimuli, study choice or unknown source exclusions.</p>
<p>Co-creation uses five participant-disjoint folds and leave-one-known-condition-out testing. Inner four-fold CV chooses ridge penalties from 0.1, 1, 10 and 100 by squared error. A shallow histogram gradient-boosting sensitivity uses 100 iterations, 3 or 7 leaves, minimum leaf size 15, L2 values 1 or 10, and no validation-based early stopping. All preprocessing and tuning remain inside training folds. Design and demographics, session logs, final adoption, and same-survey trust are compared in predefined blocks. The model does not use the free-text comments or idea semantics.</p>
<p>Latency prediction uses ten repeated five-fold participant splits stratified by the six design cells and inner three-fold tuning. Event counts receive a fixed log(1+x) transform before training-only scaling. The diagnostic rating model excludes the target item. Metrics are calculated on all out-of-fold predictions in each repeat and then averaged, not averaged over small fold-specific R² values. We report MAE, RMSE and R² on each original response scale. They are not pooled across datasets. Primary bootstrap differences condition on fitted predictions; the separate voice sensitivity described above additionally resamples and refits training data.</p>
<h3>5.3 Assignment effects and selection</h3>
<p>Voice role contrasts use linear regression with HC3 standard errors, controlling for study and style when they vary. We distinguish all released participants, first/only-check passers, and passers of every role check. Selected comparisons describe a different sample and cannot establish the effect among latent “true perceivers.” Post-treatment filtering can break randomized comparability. {cite('montgomery2018')} Paired bootstraps resample within original study × role × style cells and recompute the full and selected estimates.</p>
<p>Four exploratory role interactions with user attributes receive Holm correction. The latency analysis estimates original-scale means in a saturated 3×2 model with HC3 t/F inference. Its families are seven outcomes × three omnibus tests (21) and seven outcomes × four contrasts (28), each Holm-adjusted. Pointwise confidence intervals are labeled separately. These are different estimands and procedures from the source's aligned-rank-transform analysis; significance disagreements do not constitute a failed exact replication. All new inference is exploratory.</p>
<h3>5.4 Reproducibility checks</h3>
<p>The scripts expose input paths, seeds, sample rules, feature blocks, tuning grids and per-observation predictions. Checks cover raw hashes, unique observation keys, item range and composite agreement, note-count reconciliation, participant aggregation, complete prediction coverage, independent recalculation of prediction metrics, and rank-deficient regression designs. Independent agent reviews inspected coding, leakage risks, statistical interpretation and source provenance. Review found and repaired a constant predictor and incompatible information-criterion conventions. Such review is a quality-control step, not external peer review.</p>''')

mp=measure[measure['sample']=='pooled'].iloc[0]
add(f'''<h2>6. Results</h2><h3>6.1 Related judgments are strongly associated, without a clean factor solution</h3>
<p>In the pooled voice data, intelligence alpha is 0.882 and credibility alpha is 0.891. Composite Pearson correlation is 0.744; HTMT is 0.847 with a percentile interval [{mp.htmt_ci_low:.3f}, {mp.htmt_ci_high:.3f}]. Separate-study HTMT intervals are wider and cross 0.90. The two-factor model fits better than the one-factor model, but pooled RMSEA remains 0.105 and the latent correlation is 0.824. Thus the observations support related, potentially distinguishable judgments with unresolved residual structure. They do not establish orthogonal drivers or a well-validated two-factor measurement model.</p>''')
add(table(['Sample/model','n','χ² / df','CFI','RMSEA','Factor correlation'],[
    [r['sample']+' / '+r['model'].replace('_',' '),int(r['n']),f"{r['chi2']:.1f} / {int(r['DoF'])}",num(r['CFI']),num(r['RMSEA']),num(r['latent_correlation']) if pd.notna(r['latent_correlation']) else '—']
    for _,r in cfa.iterrows()
], 'Table 2. Continuous-item CFA diagnostics. Better relative fit does not remove substantial absolute misfit.'))
add('''<p>In text co-creation, intelligence alpha is 0.914. HTMT is 0.771 [0.626, 0.891] with capable trust, 0.775 [0.640, 0.881] with reliable trust, and 0.894 [0.830, 0.957] with the adapted likeability block. Rank-based diagnostics give the same broad picture of substantial overlap. A three-factor intelligence/capable/reliable model improves CFI to 0.947, but RMSEA is still 0.106. We do not claim that these factors are independent or that the adapted instrument has been fully validated.</p>
<p>The seven text-latency quality ratings show a large first Pearson component (eigenvalue 4.389, 62.7% of standardized variance) and alpha 0.893. A permutation parallel analysis retains one component. Ceiling-heavy ordinal responses and shared measurement can contribute; this does not turn thoughtfulness, usefulness and trustworthiness into a validated intelligence factor.</p>
<h3>6.2 Voice prediction transfers weakly from design and user information</h3>
<p>The forward design-plus-user model reaches R²=0.022, compared with 0.518 for credibility alone. Its MAE improvement over the training-mean baseline is only 0.017 points, although its conditional interval excludes zero. The user-only ablation performs somewhat better (R²=0.055), showing that adding experimental design does not guarantee improvement after tuning. In reverse assertive-only transfer, design-plus-user R²=0.026 and the MAE improvement interval crosses zero. No strong engineering predictor emerges from these limited variables.</p>''')
add(table(['Transfer/model','n test','MAE','R²','ΔMAE vs mean','95% CI for Δ'],[
    [r['split'].replace('E1_to_E2','E1 → E2').replace('E2_to_E1_assertive','E2 → E1 assertive')+' / '+r['model'].replace('_',' '),int(r.n_test),num(r.mae),num(r.r2),num(r.mae_minus_mean),f'[{r.difference_ci_low:.3f}, {r.difference_ci_high:.3f}]']
    for _,r in voice[(voice['split']!='E2_to_E1_all_shifted') & voice.model.isin(['mean','user','design_user','credibility_only'])].iterrows()
], 'Table 3. External prediction on direct intelligence ratings (1–7). Negative ΔMAE favors the model; intervals condition on fitted predictors.'))
add('''<p>Credibility alone gives MAE 0.625 in forward transfer and 0.634 in reverse transfer. This is a reproducible predictive association with another judgment from the same respondent and questionnaire. It is not evidence that credibility causes intelligence perceptions, that the system can predict these scores without questionnaires, or that a live voice assistant would retain this performance.</p>''')
add(fig('../figures/voice_transfer_prediction.png','Figure 1. Study-held-out voice prediction. Concurrent credibility is a diagnostic benchmark and is unavailable in a log-only setting.'))

add('''<h3>6.3 Available co-creation logs show limited point-prediction gains</h3>
<p>For the 142 known-assignment sessions, ridge prediction using design, demographics and available logs has participant-CV R²=−0.005; adding adoption gives 0.012. The nonlinear counterpart with adoption gives 0.023. Exploratory resampling ranges overlap zero, but their CV-dependent errors do not establish calibrated confidence coverage. The same-survey trust model gives R²=0.503 with participant holdout and 0.512 when a condition is held out. These results survive a more flexible alternative without establishing that unmeasured behavior, semantic content or higher-quality telemetry cannot predict perceptions.</p>''')
add(table(['Model','Participant R²','Condition-held-out R²','Participant RMSE'],[
    [name.replace('_',' '),num(co[(co['split']=='participant')&(co.model==name)].iloc[0].r2),num(co[(co['split']=='condition')&(co.model==name)].iloc[0].r2),num(co[(co['split']=='participant')&(co.model==name)].iloc[0].rmse)]
    for name in ['training_mean','design_user','telemetry','telemetry_adoption','nonlinear_telemetry','nonlinear_telemetry_adoption','same_survey_benchmark','nonlinear_same_survey']
], 'Table 4. Co-creation prediction on the 1–5 adapted intelligence scale. All models and both validation schemes are shown.'))
add(table(['Validation','Added to design/user','ΔR²','MAE reduction'],[[r['split'],r.model.replace('_',' '),f'{r.r2_gain:.4f}',f'{r.mae_reduction:.4f}'] for _,r in inc.iterrows()], 'Table 4b. E4 uses design/user as its comparator. These are paired point differences, not inferential confidence claims.'))
add('''<p>The added-log point gains are small and change with the validation scheme. Comparisons with the training mean are separate sanity baselines and cannot supply intervals for E4. Formal uncertainty for the design/user increment remains unestablished.</p>''')
add('''<p>The association between adoption and intelligence illustrates why in-sample results should not substitute for validation. In an adjusted exploratory regression, adoption fraction has coefficient 0.802 [0.057, 1.547], but its within-model Holm-adjusted p=0.279. The corresponding predictive block gives little held-out gain. This difference reflects the distinct questions asked by coefficient estimation and generalization, along with substantial uncertainty in a small sample. Adoption can also be a consequence of the same evaluative process. We do not treat it as objective task accuracy.</p>''')
add(f'''<p>A post-hoc lexical check concatenates only AI-authored ideas and adds character TF-IDF (3–5 grams, up to 10,000 features) within the training pipeline. Against a refitted design/user/log baseline, its paired R² increment is {lexical['participant']['delta_text_vs_design']['R2']:.6f} in participant CV and {lexical['condition']['delta_text_vs_design']['R2']:.6f} in condition holdout. The penalty grid includes 1000 and the solver is LSQR for both compared models; this reference is not numerically identical to Table 4. These negligible point increments concern one lexical representation. They do not establish that semantic quality is irrelevant, and no inferential interval is asserted.</p>''')
add(fig('../results/figures/cocreation_prediction.png','Figure 2. Ridge prediction in text co-creation. Intervals are 2,000 participant resamples of fixed out-of-fold predictions; they do not include full retraining uncertainty.'))

add('''<h3>6.4 Selection changes the apparent role effect</h3>
<p>The adjusted pooled partner-minus-servant contrast is −0.055 [−0.270, 0.159] in the 553 released eligible observations. Under the first/only-check rule, 328 participants remain and the contrast is +0.410 [0.141, 0.678]. The paired bootstrap change is +0.465 [0.275, 0.646]. This quantifies selection sensitivity, not bias relative to a known causal truth. In experiment 1, check retention is 46.4% in the partner arm versus 60.9% in the servant arm; experiment-2 first-check retention is about 69.5% in both. Equal retention rates would not alone eliminate post-treatment selection concerns.</p>
<p>We also reconstruct the published pooled rule: all role checks passed, combining 189 respondents from experiment 1 with 80 from experiment 2 (n=269). The observed group means exactly reproduce the source's published intelligence means, 4.9714 for partner and 4.4467 for servant. The adjusted contrast in this subset is +0.526 [0.223, 0.829]. The original authors already reported both full and selected analyses. Our contribution is an explicit reconstruction, paired sensitivity quantification and integration with measurement and prediction, not the discovery of an omitted full-sample result.</p>''')
chosen=effects[(effects.outcome=='intelligence')&((effects['sample']=='pooled')|(effects.selection=='all-released'))]
add(table(['Sample','Selection','n','Partner − servant','95% CI'],[[r['sample'],r.selection,int(r.n),num(r.estimate),ci(r)] for _,r in chosen.iterrows()], 'Table 5. Full and selected voice contrasts. Confidence intervals are pointwise HC3 intervals, not proof of causal subgroup effects.'))
add('''<p>A cumulative-logit sensitivity on the direct intelligent item retains the distinction between full and selected analyses. It is a one-item check under proportional odds, not ordinal validation of the full scale. None of four exploratory role-by-user interactions survives Holm adjustment. This does not establish equality of user groups or exclude smaller moderators; the ownership-frequency analysis is restricted to respondents with relevant service-use answers.</p>''')

add('''<h3>6.5 Latency effects depend on the endpoint and inferential specification</h3>
<p>In the 240-person text study, Creation receives usefulness ratings 0.353 points higher than Advice, averaged equally across assigned delays (95% CI [0.146, 0.561]). This survives both stated Holm families. Average longer-delay thoughtfulness is 0.336 points higher than at two seconds, with a pointwise interval [0.037, 0.635], but Holm p=0.718 over 28 contrasts. No delay-by-task interaction is rejected after correction. These results do not establish a zero latency effect and cannot recommend delaying voice responses.</p>''')
chosen=lc[((lc.outcome=='Rating_thoughtfulness')&(lc.contrast.isin(['9_minus_2','20_minus_2','long_minus_2'])))|((lc.outcome=='Rating_usefulness')&(lc.contrast.isin(['long_minus_2','creation_minus_advice'])))]
add(table(['Outcome/contrast','Difference','95% pointwise CI','Raw p','Holm p'],[[r.outcome.replace('Rating_','')+' / '+r.contrast.replace('_',' '),num(r.estimate),ci(r),num(r.p),num(r.p_holm_28)] for _,r in chosen.iterrows()], 'Table 6. Original-scale latency-study contrasts. Holm adjustment covers 28 planned exploratory contrasts.'))
add('''<p>Excluding the three ambiguous task IDs changes these key contrasts little. With 237 participants in prediction, telemetry R² is −0.006 for thoughtfulness and −0.017 for usefulness. Other concurrent survey ratings give 0.500 and 0.547 respectively. The replicated information-source pattern is suggestive but remains triangulation across different constructs. It is not external validation of an intelligence model on the latency dataset.</p>''')
add(fig('../results/figures/latency_ratings.png','Figure 3. Text-study cell means and pointwise 95% intervals. The complete 1–7 response scale is shown; this is not a voice experiment.'))
add('''<h3>6.6 Paired contrasts quantify the information-source gap</h3>
<p>A post-hoc analysis compares predictions for the same held-out people, using 4,000 paired bootstrap resamples. It contrasts the design-plus-user model against credibility in the voice studies, design/user/logs against trust in co-creation, and design/logs against other quality ratings in text chat. R² gain is the reduction in squared error divided by the same test-set outcome variance. Reported user traits are themselves survey data; the distinction is absence versus presence of concurrent evaluative judgments, not an absolute objective/subjective divide.</p>''')
add(table(['Validation/endpoint','n','R² gain','2.5–97.5% resampling range'],[[r.comparison,int(r.n),num(r.r2_gain_survey),f'[{r.r2_gain_low:.3f}, {r.r2_gain_high:.3f}]'] for _,r in gap.iterrows()], 'Table 7. Paired gain from concurrent evaluations over the stated comparison feature block. No pooled cross-study estimate is calculated.'))
add('''<p>All six fixed-prediction resampling ranges are above zero. For text CV, overlapping training sets make OOF errors dependent: these ranges are exploratory reweighting summaries, not calibrated 95% confidence intervals or six significance tests. Their coverage has not been established. The independent voice holdouts and separate training bootstrap provide a different uncertainty design. The observations and validation schemes overlap within datasets; these are not six independent replications. Latency contrasts use each participant's predictions averaged over CV repeats, so their R² contrast differs slightly from subtracting average per-repeat metrics. The result does not identify why concurrent ratings are informative and does not prove that richer telemetry would fail.</p>''')
add(fig('../results/figures/information_gap.png','Figure 4. Paired information-source comparisons. Each row retains its own outcome and validation unit. Ranges hold predictions fixed. Text CV error dependence is not accounted for; coverage is unestablished.'))
add('''<h3>6.7 The main gap persists when models are retrained</h3>
<p>The training-and-test bootstrap preserves the advantage of concurrent credibility in both voice transfers. In contrast, the small design-plus-user MAE advantage over the training mean has an interval spanning zero in both directions. The forward lower endpoint is only −0.0002: this borderline crossing should not be treated as a decisive significance reversal, especially with Monte Carlo uncertainty. The evidence supports a large information-source contrast and a small, uncertain operational improvement. The narrower conditional interval in Table 3 does not represent uncertainty over the whole training procedure.</p>''')
metrics_to_show={'mae_reduction_survey_vs_operational':'MAE reduction: credibility vs design/user','r2_gain_survey_vs_operational':'R² gain: credibility vs design/user','mae_reduction_operational_vs_mean':'MAE reduction: design/user vs mean'}
add(table(['Transfer / contrast','Grouped-CV reference','95% train-and-test interval'],[[r['split'].replace('_',' ')+' / '+metrics_to_show[r.metric],num(r.reference_grouped_cv),f'[{r.ci_low:.4f}, {r.ci_high:.4f}]'] for _,r in retrain[retrain.metric.isin(metrics_to_show)].iterrows()], 'Table 8. One thousand paired training-and-test bootstrap draws per transfer. Positive values favor the first named information set.'))
add('''<p>These intervals use empirical resampling within the same two studies. They are not population-wide guarantees, and the two directions reuse the source datasets. Reference estimates can differ slightly from Table 3 because grouped rather than shuffled inner folds are needed to keep duplicated bootstrap participants together. The observed robustness supports the main predictive contrast under a stronger uncertainty analysis, while leaving causation and live-voice generalization unresolved.</p>''')

add('''<h2>7. Discussion</h2>
<h3>7.1 What has been learned about perceived “smartness”</h3>
<p>The strongest finding is not a universal ranking of speed, correctness or politeness. Those variables are not jointly observed. Instead, three source releases consistently distinguish prediction from accessible conditions or coarse logs from prediction using nearby subjective judgments. The latter captures a large share of variance, while the former provides small, unstable or absent gains under the tested models. The resulting operational lesson is to state the information budget whenever reporting a perception predictor.</p>
<p>This pattern has several possible explanations. The available logs omit whether an answer was actually right, contextually apt or successfully repaired. Participants may combine several cues into a common evaluation. Items such as competent, knowledgeable and trustworthy overlap in meaning. A shared response style can also correlate judgments. Our designs cannot separate these explanations. Calling the gap “common-method bias” as an established cause would overstate the evidence; calling credibility an objective driver would make the opposite error.</p>
<p>The measurement results sharpen the interpretation. High reliability coexists with substantial between-scale association and residual factor-model misfit. Multiple dimensions may be useful without being independent, and a high alpha does not determine how many mechanisms exist. Researchers should report absolute fit, residual limitations and cross-group equivalence before interpreting a latent score as the same quantity across scenarios. The data do not support fixed universal factor weights for contemporary spoken assistants.</p>
<h3>7.2 Context, user differences and causal interpretation</h3>
<p>Context enters both the theoretical specification and validation. Holding out a whole voice experiment or co-creation condition tests a different generalization claim from random participant folds. In these releases, strong contemporaneous survey association transfers more readily than design-based prediction. This does not establish that the psychological construct itself is invariant. The voice experiments share a laboratory, country and stimulus family, while the co-creation conditions share a task and system.</p>
<p>User-only information has limited forward predictive value, and the tested interactions are uncertain. That is insufficient to conclude that user characteristics do not matter. More precise prior-experience measures, accessibility needs, language proficiency and repeated-use history may be important, but this study cannot estimate them. The fitted demographic coefficients are descriptive; they are not a recommendation to personalize treatment based on protected categories.</p>
<p>Selection deserves equal attention to model fit. A model of perceived intelligence often contains recognition checks or user interpretations that occur after exposure. Excluding participants based on those measures can alter the role contrast markedly. Full-release and selected analyses answer different questions. Reproducing a selected-sample effect is valuable, but it does not recover a population assignment effect or identify how much of a treatment's influence passes through recognition.</p>
<h3>7.3 Implications for evaluation practice</h3>
<p>For a product evaluation, collect direct intelligence judgments separately from satisfaction and trust. Preserve objective success/correctness and timing even when users report a good experience. Declare which inputs are available before, during and after a session. Compare against a training-mean baseline and a simple established model before introducing a complex architecture. Use participant, scenario and system holdouts, with all tuning confined to training. Treat same-survey predictors as a separate diagnostic analysis.</p>
<p>These recommendations do not imply that perception should be maximized regardless of actual capability. A system can become more persuasive without becoming more correct. The present evidence supports tracking perceived competence and verified performance as separate outcomes; their alignment requires new data. Neither the null telemetry result nor an attractive subjective score justifies suppressing errors, adding artificial delays or replacing correctness checks with a perception metric.</p>

<h2>8. Limitations and unperformed tests</h2>
<p><strong>Modality and ecological validity.</strong> The only direct voice-specific outcome comes from observers of video vignettes. There are no live speech-recognition errors, interruptions or per-turn repair measurements. The actual interaction datasets are text/canvas and text chat. Claims about real-time spoken interaction remain prospective.</p>
<p><strong>Measurement.</strong> Instruments differ in item wording, response range and administration. Continuous covariance models have poor absolute fit. Full ordinal CFA, measurement invariance, test–retest stability, Russian translation validation and a distinction between general halo and causal mechanisms were not completed. The latent specification is a falsifiable proposal; its complete parameters were not estimated.</p>
<p><strong>Available information.</strong> No analyzed release jointly measures correctness, task success, true response latency, repair quality, user expectations and intelligence. Coarse event counts and surviving-note statistics may be weak proxies. Language-model semantic encoders and large neural predictors were not evaluated; the negative result is limited to the specified features, ridge models and the co-creation nonlinear sensitivity.</p>
<p><strong>Sampling and inference.</strong> Public releases already include exclusions. Six co-creation assignments are unknown and three latency IDs are ambiguous. The source studies are not a probability sample of assistant users. Primary conditional intervals omit model-training uncertainty; the voice retraining sensitivity addresses empirical training variation but not study or stimulus sampling. Co-creation and latency were not subjected to full retraining bootstraps; row-resampling of their fixed OOF predictions does not establish confidence coverage for dependent CV errors. Exploratory findings require independent confirmation. No prospective sample-size calculation can retroactively make this secondary analysis confirmatory.</p>
<p><strong>Novelty and review.</strong> The literature search is broad but not exhaustive. Several access-limited novelty leads remain documented. The methods are established; the new contribution is their concrete empirical application and linked findings. No external peer review, venue acceptance or blanket “first-ever” claim is made. A strong submission would benefit from prospectively collected live-voice evidence and human domain-expert review.</p>

<h2>9. Prospective validation and pilot boundary</h2>
<p>The accompanying prospective protocol specifies the missing study: directly interact with multiple voice systems, independently manipulate task evidence where feasible, retain all eligible randomized participants, collect direct perception items, and preserve participant × scenario × system structure. A Russian pilot pack provides matched multi-turn prompts, a local rating form and a separate scoring key. It is ready for later human use; no pilot observations are included in this manuscript.</p>
<p>Conversations conducted by an automated agent can test response behavior and the collection pipeline. They cannot supply a human's perceived-intelligence score. A single person's multi-model sessions form a usability and instrumentation pilot; repeating tasks does not create independent participants. Model comparisons are also bundles of voice, interface, instructions and model version unless those elements are controlled. The pilot will therefore be stored separately from the public-data results and cannot silently convert this study into a population experiment.</p>

<h2>10. Conclusion</h2>
<p>We complete a reproducible secondary empirical study of directly measured perceived intelligence and related outcomes, with explicit measurement, prediction and selection checks. Design and limited behavioral logs provide much less predictive information than contemporaneous subjective judgments in these releases. Correlated-factor models improve relative fit but do not validate independent components, and role effects depend materially on post-treatment selection. The evidence supports a model whose interpretation depends on context and information availability. It does not support a universal engineering score for live voice-assistant “smartness.” The next decisive test requires the measurements that the available datasets do not jointly contain.</p>

<h2>Transparency and artifacts</h2>
<p>The repository includes raw-source manifests and downloader, pinned Python dependencies, three analysis programs, per-participant predictions, complete numerical outputs, an inference example, literature and review records, and the prospective pilot. No new data, quotations, source identities or effect estimates were fabricated. AI assistance was used for literature retrieval, programming, analysis and drafting; authorship, ethical approval and external review are not implied. Raw-data rights remain with their source authors.</p>
<p>Analysis date: 4 November 2026. Primary seed: 20261104; training-bootstrap seed: 202611041. Reproducibility entry point: <code>python scripts/run_all.py</code>. The article tables are generated from saved results, while all conclusions are checked against those same outputs. Detailed sensitivity tables remain available even when not printed in the main article.</p>
<h2>References</h2>''')
